The AssetRegistry module exposes `IAssetRegistry` with a synchronous GetAssets(Filter, OutAssets) call:

```cpp
FAssetRegistryModule& AssetRegistryModule =
    FModuleManager::LoadModuleChecked<FAssetRegistryModule>("AssetRegistry");
IAssetRegistry& AssetRegistry = AssetRegistryModule.Get();

FARFilter Filter;
Filter.ClassPaths.Add(UBlueprint::StaticClass()->GetClassPathName());
Filter.bRecursiveClasses = true;
Filter.bRecursivePaths = true;
Filter.PackagePaths.Add(FName("/Game"));

TArray<FAssetData> AssetList;
AssetRegistry.GetAssets(Filter, AssetList);

for (const FAssetData& Asset : AssetList)
{
    UBlueprint* BP = Cast<UBlueprint>(Asset.GetAsset());
    if (BP && BP->ParentClass && BP->ParentClass->IsChildOf(AActor::StaticClass()))
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Log, TEXT("Actor BP: %s"), *Asset.AssetName.ToString());
    }
}
```

UE5 vs UE4 notes: use `ClassPaths` (FName via GetClassPathName()) not the deprecated `ClassNames` (FString). The ClassPaths API uses FTopLevelAssetPath, which is safe before classes are loaded.

To avoid forcing the BP class to load (slow), use the lazy path: read `FBlueprintTags::NativeParentClassPath` from Asset.TagsAndValues (an FString), parse the asset path, and check inheritance without loading.

In a custom AActor subclass, you typically have a UBoxComponent (or USphereComponent) for the trigger volume. Pattern:

```cpp
// MyTriggerActor.h
UCLASS()
class AMyTriggerActor : public AActor
{
    GENERATED_BODY()
public:
    AMyTriggerActor();

    UFUNCTION()
    void OnOverlapBegin(UPrimitiveComponent* OverlappedComp, AActor* OtherActor,
                        UPrimitiveComponent* OtherComp, int32 OtherBodyIndex,
                        bool bFromSweep, const FHitResult& SweepResult);

private:
    UPROPERTY(VisibleAnywhere)
    TObjectPtr<class UBoxComponent> TriggerBox;
};
```

```cpp
// MyTriggerActor.cpp
AMyTriggerActor::AMyTriggerActor()
{
    PrimaryActorTick.bCanEverTick = false;
    TriggerBox = CreateDefaultSubobject<UBoxComponent>(TEXT("TriggerBox"));
    TriggerBox->SetBoxExtent(FVector(200.f));
    TriggerBox->SetCollisionProfileName(TEXT("Trigger"));
    RootComponent = TriggerBox;
}

void AMyTriggerActor::BeginPlay()
{
    Super::BeginPlay();
    TriggerBox->OnComponentBeginOverlap.AddDynamic(this, &AMyTriggerActor::OnOverlapBegin);
}

void AMyTriggerActor::OnOverlapBegin(UPrimitiveComponent*, AActor* OtherActor,
                                     UPrimitiveComponent*, int32, bool, const FHitResult&)
{
    if (OtherActor && OtherActor != this)
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Log, TEXT("%s overlapped trigger"), *OtherActor->GetName());
    }
}
```

Key requirements:
- Handler function MUST be a UFUNCTION() — AddDynamic uses reflection to bind.
- Signature must match FComponentBeginOverlapSignature exactly: 6 parameters of those types.
- Set the collision profile to "Trigger" (or a custom overlap-all-but-no-blocking profile) so pawns overlap instead of being blocked.
- AddDynamic in BeginPlay, not in the constructor — the component must be fully registered first.

The cleanest, type-safe pattern is a BlueprintAssignable dynamic multicast delegate:

```cpp
// MyActor.h
DECLARE_DYNAMIC_MULTICAST_DELEGATE_OneParam(FOnHealthChanged, float, NewHealth);

UCLASS()
class AMyActor : public AActor
{
    GENERATED_BODY()
public:
    UPROPERTY(BlueprintAssignable, Category = "Events")
    FOnHealthChanged OnHealthChanged;

    UFUNCTION(BlueprintCallable, Category = "Stats")
    void TakeDamage(float Amount);

    UPROPERTY(EditAnywhere, BlueprintReadWrite, Category = "Stats")
    float Health = 100.f;
};
```

```cpp
// MyActor.cpp
void AMyActor::TakeDamage(float Amount)
{
    Health = FMath::Max(0.f, Health - Amount);
    OnHealthChanged.Broadcast(Health);
}
```

In Blueprint, drag from the actor reference, find "Bind Event to On Health Changed", connect a custom event. Any BP that has this actor bound can now react to damage.

The wrong way is to call BP functions directly by name (FindFunction + ProcessEvent). It works but bypasses the type system, makes refactoring painful, and shows up as a stringly-typed dependency in the BP graph.

For one-shot events (e.g. "OnDied"), use BlueprintImplementableEvent (C++ declares, BP provides body) or BlueprintNativeEvent (C++ default + BP override).
